This is the opening page, §1 'Courbes standard', of Grothendieck's manuscript on the structure at infinity of the moduli spaces M_{g,ν}. Its purpose is to fix the class of objects whose combinatorics (the 'graph associated to a standard curve', §2) will later index the boundary strata of the Mumford–Deligne compactification.

Definition. Let k be algebraically closed. A 'standard curve' over k is a k-scheme X such that:
(a) X is quasi-projective and every irreducible component has dimension 1;
(b) every point of X is either smooth or an ordinary quadratic point, i.e. étale-locally isomorphic to the origin of Spec k[X,Y]/(XY).
In modern terms X is a possibly non-proper, possibly disconnected nodal curve: an open (punctured) nodal curve.

Completion. There is a completion X̂ with:
- X̂ proper;
- X a dense open subscheme of X̂;
- X̂ smooth at the points of I = X̂ \ X.
Then X̂ is a projective curve, and I is a finite subset of X̂(k) contained in the smooth locus. The singular set A of X equals that of X̂, giving display (1):
- I = X̂ \ X ⊂ X̂^lisse(k), the 'points at infinity';
- A = X \ X^lisse = X̂ \ X̂^lisse ⊂ X(k), the nodes.

Equivalence of data. The page ends mid-sentence, and the next page completes it: giving X is equivalent to giving a pair (X̂, I), where X̂ is a projective nodal curve and I is a finite set of smooth points. This is exactly the datum of a ν-pointed (unordered) nodal curve, as in Deligne–Mumford–Knudsen. In other words, Grothendieck chooses the 'open curve' viewpoint (punctured curves, whose fundamental groups will matter later) rather than the 'marked proper curve' viewpoint. The next page continues with the normalization, the preimage Ã of the nodes, and the fixed-point-free involution on Ã. These are the ingredients of the dual graph.
